<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    docs/plan/03 § build/ — Nuke, the single entry point for every build action.

    This project is deliberately NOT a member of CyberCloud.slnx. If it were, `Compile` would build
    the build system while the build system is building it. See docs/plan/23 § Build.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsPublishable>false</IsPublishable>

    <!--
      Nuke's convention: the build definition lives in the global namespace so `Build.cs` reads the
      same in every Nuke repository. The root Directory.Build.props sets RootNamespace to the project
      name, which would be `_build` — not a namespace we want in the generated assembly attributes.
    -->
    <RootNamespace></RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>_build</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Where Nuke considers the repository root and where it writes the bootstrapping scripts.
      Both are the repository root, one level up.
    -->
    <NukeRootDirectory>..</NukeRootDirectory>
    <NukeScriptDirectory>..</NukeScriptDirectory>

    <!--
      ⚠ TELEMETRY: this property is what stops Nuke asking on the console.

      Verified by extracting the UTF-16 string literals from Nuke.Build.dll 10.1.0 — the notice and
      its escape hatches are all there:

        "NUKE collects anonymous usage data in order to help us improve your experience."
        "Read more about scope, data points, and opt-out: https://nuke.build/telemetry"
        "Press <Enter> to " + "create awareness cookie for " / "set the NukeTelemetryVersion property"
        "Run in interactive console to fix this warning"
        "NUKE_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT"

      Nuke's own remedy is either a per-user cookie file or this MSBuild property. The property is
      the one that belongs in the repository: a cookie is per-machine, so a CI runner with a clean
      home directory would meet the question again. `build.sh` / `build.cmd` additionally export
      NUKE_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT=1 for anyone invoking `dotnet run` or `dotnet nuke` by hand.

      ⚠ This string does NOT appear in Nuke.Common.dll — Nuke 10 is componentised and the
      telemetry lives in Nuke.Build.dll. Grepping the wrong assembly is how you conclude, wrongly,
      that there is no telemetry to opt out of.
    -->
    <NukeTelemetryVersion>1</NukeTelemetryVersion>

    <!--
      Nuke's target fields are assigned by reflection and its own generated members are not all
      consumed, which trips a family of "this is never used / could be static" diagnostics across
      the whole build definition.

      Directory.Build.targets already scopes `TreatWarningsAsErrors=false` and
      `NoWarn=CA1515;CA1822;CS0649` to this project (IsBuildProject). The additions below are the
      ones that survived that and were observed failing a real `dotnet build build/_build.csproj`:

        CS0169  — private field is never used            (`[Parameter]` fields read by reflection)
        CA1812  — internal class is never instantiated   (the Build class is instantiated by Nuke)
        IDE0044 — make field readonly                    (Nuke assigns [Parameter] fields post-ctor)
        IDE0051 — private member is unused               (same reflection story as CS0169)

        CA1707  — "Remove the underscores from assembly name _build". `_build` is Nuke's fixed
                  convention for the build project; `nuke`'s own tooling and every Nuke repository
                  expect it. This one is suppressed here rather than in build/.editorconfig because
                  it is an assembly-level diagnostic with no meaningful source file to scope to —
                  it was reported against whichever .cs file happened to sort first.

      ⚠ NoWarn alone is not enough for the rules the root .editorconfig sets to `severity = error`
      (IDE1006, IDE0005, …) — that severity is applied regardless. Those are handled in
      build/.editorconfig, which says which rule and why for each.

      This relaxation is scoped to build/ on purpose. Do NOT move any of it into the root
      Directory.Build.props — product code must keep every one of these as an error.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0169;CA1812;IDE0044;IDE0051;CA1707</NoWarn>

    <!--
      Directory.Build.targets scopes `TreatWarningsAsErrors=false` to this project, but
      Directory.Build.props also sets `CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors=true`, which is a separate
      switch covering the CAxxxx family and is NOT overridden there. Without this line the exemption
      build/README.md advertises only half works — verified: `dotnet build build/_build.csproj`
      failed with `error CA1707` and `error CA1852` while TreatWarningsAsErrors was already false.
    -->
    <CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      ⚠ NO Version= ATTRIBUTE. Central Package Management is on repo-wide
      (Directory.Packages.props), and an inline version is NU1008 and a hard restore failure.
      Nuke's own `nuke :setup` template writes the version inline — if you regenerate this file from
      a template, strip it again. The pin lives in Directory.Packages.props § Build.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Nuke.Common" />

    <!--
      ⚠ FOR ONE GATE, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THAT GATE — issue #73, `Generated SDK compiles`.
      Nothing in this repository compiled `generated/sdk/*.cs`, so FOUR defect families shipped
      inside a checked-in .NET SDK while the `Generated surfaces` row compared it byte-for-byte and
      passed: one CS0101, one CS0246, seventeen CS0102s and 110 CS9035s. "Is this valid C#" is a
      question only a C# compiler answers; GeneratedSdkSurface.cs hands each file to Roslyn, and a
      brace counter or a regular expression would be the same not-a-compiler the gate exists to
      replace. Same argument, same package, as CyberCloud.Kubernetes.Contracts.Tests
      § CompileFailureTests.

      ⚠ This said "three defects" until issue #81 — one of three sentences in build/ that the 484bacf
      review left a family short while it was correcting their CS0102 count. GeneratedSdkSurface.cs
      § GeneratedSdkSurface shows how all four counts were derived, and from which blob.

      ⚠ NO Version= — CPM is on repo-wide and the 5.6.0 pin lives in Directory.Packages.props
      § Roslyn, where its ceiling (the compiler that loads CyberCloud.Analyzers) is argued. This
      project is not an analyzer and does not set that ceiling; it shares the pin because CPM has one
      version per package id. 5.6.0 is the released build of the compiler in the 10.0.3xx SDK band —
      the band global.json now floors at — and NOT necessarily the csc of the SDK running the build:
      on 10.0.400 that csc is 5.9.0-1.26379.115 (issue #80). Compiling the generated file against the
      floor rather than against whatever the developer happens to have installed is the behaviour this
      gate wants anyway: a consumer on the oldest supported SDK is the reader it speaks for.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
